---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifePairConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifePairConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="meters-to-feet">
  <UnitsLifePairConverter client:load spec="meters-to-feet" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Decimal feet is rarely the answer you need</h2>
    <p>
      Meters to feet gives a number like 5.7415. That is correct but rarely useful. Nobody describes a
      person as five point seven four feet tall, and no builder marks out 0.74 of a foot. You want
      <strong>5 ft 8.9 in</strong>, and for that you convert the fractional part separately.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page shows both. Use decimal feet for spreadsheets and CAD, and feet and inches for the
      rest. The remainder carries correctly, so a value that rounds up to twelve inches goes into the
      next foot, and you never see "5 ft 12 in".
    </p>

    <h2>The arithmetic</h2>
    <p>
      A foot is exactly 0.3048 m, so metres to feet is a division by 0.3048, or a multiplication by
      3.280839895. To split the result:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Take the whole number of feet. For 1.75 m, 5.7415 ft gives 5 ft.</li>
      <li>Take what is left, 0.7415 ft, and multiply by 12 to get inches: 8.898 in.</li>
      <li>Round the inches to the precision you need: 8.9 in, or 9 in to the nearest whole inch.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Human heights in both systems</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Metres</th><th scope="col">Centimetres</th><th scope="col">Feet and inches</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1.524</td><td>152.4</td><td>5 ft 0 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1.60</td><td>160</td><td>5 ft 3 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1.65</td><td>165</td><td>5 ft 5 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1.70</td><td>170</td><td>5 ft 6.9 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1.75</td><td>175</td><td>5 ft 8.9 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1.80</td><td>180</td><td>5 ft 10.9 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1.83</td><td>183</td><td>6 ft 0 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1.90</td><td>190</td><td>6 ft 2.8 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2.00</td><td>200</td><td>6 ft 6.7 in</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Six foot is 1.8288 m. So "1.83 m" is the standard way to write it, and 1.80 m is five eleven, not
      six foot.
    </p>

    <h2>Building dimensions worth knowing</h2>
    <p>
      A standard UK or European ceiling at 2.4 m is 7 ft 10.5 in. The American 8 ft equivalent is
      2.44 m. A 2.1 m internal door is 6 ft 10.7 in, and the US standard is 6 ft 8 in (2.032 m), so
      imported doors rarely fit an existing frame. Scaffold boards, worktops and plasterboard come in
      metric lengths that are close to, but never equal to, the old imperial lengths.
    </p>

    <h2>The survey foot footnote</h2>
    <p>
      Until 2023, US land surveying used a slightly different foot: 1200/3937 m, about two parts per
      million longer than the international foot. Over a metre that is 0.002 mm and does not matter.
      Over a state plane coordinate of several hundred thousand feet, it is metres. The survey foot is
      now officially retired, and this page uses the international foot throughout.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
